WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:02.800
CARTER MORGAN: This week, we're
talking about the Kubernetes

00:00:02.800 --> 00:00:05.740
product security committee,
Java 11 for App Engine,

00:00:05.740 --> 00:00:07.890
and GKE usage metering.

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:10.240
I'm Carter Morgan, and
welcome to This Week in Cloud,

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.270
where we serve you the
lowest latency news.

00:00:13.270 --> 00:00:14.890
When it comes to
open source software,

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:16.960
there's plenty of
work to be done.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:19.450
That can be both
encouraging and terrifying,

00:00:19.450 --> 00:00:21.250
depending on your perspective.

00:00:21.250 --> 00:00:23.000
If you're a company
considering Kubernetes

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.120
as the core of your
cloud strategy,

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:26.920
this recap of security
investigations

00:00:26.920 --> 00:00:30.102
should be a great comfort
to you and your CTO.

00:00:30.102 --> 00:00:32.560
It's a great breakdown of how
vulnerabilities are assessed,

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:35.050
investigated, and resolved
by the product security

00:00:35.050 --> 00:00:37.000
committee for Kubernetes.

00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:39.850
Speaking of resolving things,
Java 11 for App Engine

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:41.750
is now generally available.

00:00:41.750 --> 00:00:44.350
So if you've been dreaming of
building modern Java backends,

00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:46.330
using modern frameworks,
or simply using

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:48.280
the latest language
features of Java 11,

00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:50.980
your dreams have come true.

00:00:50.980 --> 00:00:53.530
Also new this week, more
features in BigQuery.

00:00:53.530 --> 00:00:55.060
We've added new
scripting and stored

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:57.670
procedures, multiple queries
in a single statement,

00:00:57.670 --> 00:01:00.610
simplified the process for
migrating Teradata to BigQuery,

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:02.650
and increased BI
Engine reservations.

00:01:02.650 --> 00:01:05.140
These updates bring even
more flexibility, simplicity,

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.420
and choice to your
data warehouse.

00:01:07.420 --> 00:01:10.690
As always, Google Cloud is
designed to be flexible.

00:01:10.690 --> 00:01:13.952
And this means making sure your
Cloud usage fits your needs.

00:01:13.952 --> 00:01:15.910
To help out with this,
Google Kubernetes Engine

00:01:15.910 --> 00:01:19.160
rolled out Usage Metering
in beta earlier this year.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:20.680
It's now ready
for anyone to use.

00:01:20.680 --> 00:01:23.620
So you can reduce waste thanks
to consumption-based metrics

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:26.470
that allow you to compare and
contrast resource requests

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:28.107
with actual utilization.

00:01:28.107 --> 00:01:30.190
If you want to read more
about these announcements

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:32.340
or see a full list,
check out the link.

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.010
And if you have any
thoughts or ideas,

00:01:34.010 --> 00:01:35.560
leave us a comment or tweet us.

00:01:35.560 --> 00:01:36.640
I'm Carter Morgan.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:38.820
And we'll see you in the Cloud.

